{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 2.} The eight benchmark splits, over seven datasets \u8212- brain appears twice because the same nuclei are scored at two levels of one taxonomy. {\i split} separates within-dataset holdouts from cross-dataset and cross-study transfer; {\i genes} records whether the matrix is keyed by Ensembl ids or by gene symbols, since a method or reference built on one convention cannot read the other without remapping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The blood+gut set is a subsample of {\b IBDverse} (Wellcome Sanger Institute; blood, terminal ileum and rectum from 421 individuals), included here because 86 fine-grained labels make it the high-cardinality stress case for the panel. It carries no Cell Ontology ids, so it contributes to accuracy and macro-F1 but to no ontology-scored result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The brain set is single nuclei from the Allen Institute\u8217's human {\b middle temporal gyrus} taxonomy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ade9516"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Jorstad 2023]
}}}
, the same query used in the multi-reference agreement experiment of \u167 ?3.5. It enters twice, at two levels of that taxonomy: {\b {\f1 Subclass}} (24 types, ~300 cells each), the standard annotation level, and {\b {\f1 Cluster}} (151 types, ~100 cells each), whose members are the cell sets {\f1 CCN201908210} enumerates. Only the 10x 3\u8242 ? v3 nuclei are kept (141,782 of 156,285); the remaining Smart-seq v4 nuclei would put a full-length protocol on one side of a within-dataset split.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both levels are scored by exact match alone. The Cell Ontology collapses the five intratelencephalic subclasses ({\f1 L2/3 IT}, {\f1 L4 IT}, {\f1 L5 IT}, {\f1 L6 IT}, {\f1 L6 IT Car3}) into one term and merges {\f1 Lamp5}/{\f1 Lamp5_Lhx6} and {\f1 Sst}/{\f1 Sst Chodl}, leaving {\b 18 terms for the 24 subclasses} and none for the 151 clusters, so an ontology-aware score would credit a method for confusing cortical layers. Brain therefore contributes accuracy and macro-F1 and no ontology-scored result, as blood+gut does for a different reason. \u167 ?3.5 returns to this gap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The set spans the three generalization regimes (within-dataset, across datasets, across studies), more than an order of magnitude in cell-type count (8\u8594 ?151), five tissues (lung, liver, blood, gut, brain), whole cells and single nuclei, and both gene-ID conventions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metrics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Per (dataset, method, repeat): {\b accuracy}; {\b macro-F1}, unweighted over classes so rare types count as much as common ones; {\b ontology-aware concordance}; {\b fit time}, {\b predict time}, and {\b peak memory} (peak RSS, resident set size \u8212- the maximum physical memory the process held).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Ontology-aware concordance} credits a call that is the same node, an ancestor, or a descendant of the truth in the Cell Ontology. It exists because cell-type vocabularies disagree about granularity more than about biology: {\f1 periportal region hepatocyte} against {\f1 Hepatocyte} is a right-lineage-wrong-depth call. Exact match scores it as a total failure, though the lineage is correct and only the depth is wrong. It is the {\b only} metric that compares methods with different label vocabularies (\u167 ?2.2), and it is reported only where both reference and query carry CL ids \u8212- so not on {\f1 blood_gut_intra}, and not on the symbol-keyed {\f1 pbmc}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concordance depends on the ontology release, since ancestor sets change between them. All numbers here use the {\b Cell Ontology basic release of 2026-06-08} (sha256 73996c63\u8230 ?), resolved once and reused by every scoring pass. Scoring lung_cross against a later-fetched release reproduces the splits exactly (14,390 reference / 13,550 query cells) and still moves concordance by 0.003, with no model changing. That release, its checksum, and pinned versions for all six benchmark environments are recorded as lockfiles in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/iandriver/actinn-jax-benchmark"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
benchmark repository
}}}
, which also carries a command that reports any drift from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Exact-match accuracy is reported for the ten reference-trained methods and, on {\f1 lung_cross}, flagged: reference and query use different label vocabularies there, so its exact-match accuracy is a vocabulary artifact and the ranking, not the level, is what transfers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Harness, isolation, and hardware\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each method runs in {\b its own virtual environment, as a separate process}, because the dependency sets are mutually unsatisfiable \u8212- scVI-based methods, TensorFlow/Keras (Pan-human Azimuth), R (SingleR, scmap) and the JAX core cannot coexist in one interpreter. One driver builds the reference/query split {\b once per dataset} from a fixed seed and hands the identical pair to every method, so no method sees a different split. {\b repeats = 3}; scPRINT and Pan-human Azimuth run once each, being deterministic given a fixed query and dominated by a single forward pass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resource accounting is per subprocess: fit and predict are timed separately and peak memory is sampled by a monitor thread in the child, so one method\u8217's footprint cannot be attributed to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Hardware}: Apple Silicon (M-series), CPU for the classical, linear, correlation and pretrained tiers and for actinn-jax; Apple MPS for the deep and foundation tiers. No discrete GPU, no cloud \u8212- the regime the benchmark is about. Math-library threads are left uncapped, so wall-clock is what a practitioner sees; an optional thread cap exists for stricter reproducibility. \u167 ?3.3 repeats the comparison on one cloud CPU instance, where methods compete for cores; \u167 ?2.10 explains how that is handled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Building a broad reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bundled broad-pass references (\u167 ?3.5) are built once, offline, by a three-stage pipeline driven by one command in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/iandriver/actinn-jax-benchmark"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
benchmark repository
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sampling.} Every primary cell for one organism in the CELLxGENE Census {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chanzuckerberg.github.io/cellxgene-census/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[CZI Census 2025]
}}}
 \u8212- primary meaning the copy the census designates canonical, so cells deposited in more than one study are counted once \u8212- stratified by cell type at a fixed cap per type: 40 for {\f1 broad_human_v1}, 60 for the later human and mouse pulls. Types with fewer than 12 cells are dropped, as are labels that carry no information (\u8220"unknown\u8221", \u8220"native cell\u8221", \u8220"eukaryotic cell\u8221", \u8220"animal cell\u8221"). The pull is checkpointed every ten datasets and retried, since transient object-store read errors are routine at this scale. Genes are carried under both Ensembl ids and symbols, because a symbol-keyed consumer cannot use an Ensembl-only pull. The census \u8220"stable\u8221" alias is a moving pointer, so the release it resolved to is recorded; every reference here comes from {\b census 2025-11-08}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Coarse hierarchy \u8212- two routes.} The model is a coarse classifier over groups plus one fine classifier per group, and the grouping comes from either:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Foundation-model embeddings.} scPRINT (medium-v1.5) embeds the reference once in 4,000-cell chunks; per-cell-type centroids are Ward-clustered into roughly \u8730 ?(number of types) groups, with a floor of eight. scPRINT\u8217's QC drops cells, so the embedding is not positionally aligned to the input and the label is carried through the model with the vectors. This is the only step that wants a GPU/MPS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Cell Ontology lineage.} Each cell type is described by a binary indicator over the CL terms it descends from; terms appearing in fewer than two types, or in all of them, are dropped as uninformative; the same Ward clustering is applied to those vectors. Free, deterministic, and species-independent \u8212- which is what makes the pan-mouse reference possible, since the distillation teacher is human-only and scPRINT\u8217's mouse support is untested here. \u167 ?3.5 scores this route against no hierarchy and against a random grouping.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Training and calibration.} A 4,000-gene panel of {\b highly variable genes (HVGs)} \u8212- the genes that differ most across cells, and so carry most of the signal for telling types apart \u8212- is selected on the reference; the coarse and per-group fine models are trained on it. Abstain calibration holds out {\b 10% of cell types entirely} as out-of-distribution (OOD \u8212- types the model was never trained on, standing in for the novel populations a real query contains), plus a 20% within-type test split, and sweeps the confidence threshold to trade coverage against accuracy on kept cells \u8212- the table each bundled reference reports.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Provenance and verification.} Each reference carries a build record: census release, corpus sizes, hierarchy route, organism and calibration table. Because a rebuild can train cleanly and annotate {\i worse}, the previous reference is backed up and the new one is scored on a held-out atlas before it is kept. For {\f1 broad_mouse_v1} the test set is carved from the census itself: two datasets are excluded from the reference sample and re-pulled as the query, so \u8220"held out\u8221" means held-out {\i datasets} and not merely held-out cells.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Distilling a pretrained annotator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A broad reference can also be built without labels and without a GPU, by taking both the label vocabulary and the hierarchy from an existing pretrained annotator. Keras/TensorFlow and JAX cannot share a process, so this runs in two stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Teacher pass.} Pan-human Azimuth labels a corpus of raw counts through its low-level API in batches of 8,192, which loads the weights once rather than once per call. All eight levels are retained. Where the package cannot reconcile a refined level with its coarser ones it blanks that column; those cells still carry a deepest-level call, so we fall back to it rather than discard them \u8212- dropping them would distill only the cells the teacher found easy. Predictions are mapped to CL ids through the package\u8217's own crosswalk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Student pass.} The student is a hierarchical actinn-jax reference whose {\b classes} are the teacher\u8217's refined fine labels (including its trained {\f1 Unassigned} quality-control class) and whose {\b hierarchy} is the teacher\u8217's own broad level \u8212- so the coarse\u8594 ?fine structure is inherited rather than rediscovered. 4,000-gene HVG panel; classes with fewer than 8 cells dropped, since a class that cannot be split into train and test makes fidelity depend on which side its cells landed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Corpus.} Three local atlases (lung, liver, blood+gut) capped at 600 cells per label, plus a census-wide pull (\u8804 ?60 per type). The atlases\u8217' own labels are used {\b only for scoring}, never for training.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Evaluation.} Two arms answer different questions. {\i In-corpus} holds out a stratified 25% of cells and measures {\b fidelity} \u8212- how often the student reproduces the teacher, exactly and ontology-equivalently. {\i Held-out atlas} withholds an entire atlas and measures whether the student generalizes to data the corpus never covered. Both arms also score student and teacher against the atlases\u8217' own labels, which separates distillation loss from teacher error. Once the census pull is in the corpus, a withheld {\i atlas} is no longer a withheld {\i tissue}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refinement, abstention, and novelty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four mechanisms operate on a trained reference at inference time, all label-free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Confidence abstention.} Cells whose final probability is below a threshold are relabeled \u8220"unknown\u8221" rather than forced to the nearest class. Calibrated per reference (\u167 ?2.6).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Refinement to the query.} The reference\u8217's class set is masked to those classes the query\u8217's own predictions actually support, and the softmax renormalized \u8212- no ground truth, no retraining.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Refinement to a tissue.} Classes are masked to those the census records in the given tissue, while {\b pan-tissue} types (immune, endothelial, stromal) stay available everywhere, so liver-resident T cells are unaffected. The per-class tissue map is baked into the bundled reference; the tissue is given explicitly or read from the query\u8217's own metadata, with common synonyms (PBMC\u8594 ?blood, hepatic\u8594 ?liver) recognized. A tissue outside the reference\u8217's vocabulary imposes no filter rather than an empty one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cluster-level novelty screen.} A cell type absent from the reference appears not as scattered uncertain cells but as a {\i coherent group the reference cannot confidently explain}. Clusters that are both large enough to be a real population and predominantly low-confidence are flagged, with marker genes reported for each. This is distinct from per-cell abstention.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Within-type state.} The same machinery resolves structure below a cell-type label: a hepatocyte zonation model trained on portal\u8594 ?central zone labels, scored by within-one-zone agreement across donors and datasets.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling protocol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A benchmark run at one reference size measures that size, not the method. Since a reference can be anything from a few thousand cells to a whole atlas, we subsample the reference and hold the query fixed, so accuracy, fit time, predict time and peak memory become functions of reference size alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each sweep answers a different question. {\b Cost against size and cardinality} (\u167 ?3.4, Figure 2) covers six reference sizes to 24k cells and separately varies the number of cell types, and asks whether inference cost grows with either \u8212- the property the multi-stage workflow depends on. {\b Accuracy and memory to atlas scale} covers five sizes to 49k cells on lung and four to 47k on the HLiCA liver atlas (\u167 ?3.4, Figure 3), and asks whether the ranking at laptop size survives at atlas size. A third sweep runs the other way, holding the reference fixed and growing the {\i query} to a whole 525k-cell atlas (\u167 ?3.4, Figure 4), which is the axis a reused reference is actually pointed along. Sizes are set by capping cells per type, which is why they are not round numbers. The lung sweep\u8217's top point is the whole atlas once the query is held out; the liver atlas is far larger (525k cells), so its sweep stops at a matching reference size rather than at its own ceiling, which is what lets the two be read against each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 External validation protocol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Open Problems {\f1 label_projection}} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openproblems.bio"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Open Problems 2024]
}}}
 supplies the datasets, metrics and ranking \u8212- none chosen by us. actinn-jax is packaged as a viash 0.9.7 component declaring the normalization it expects, and run through the project\u8217's own Nextflow workflow. We additionally built components for four baselines (linear-anova-pca, scTOP, SVM, CellTypist) so the same-hardware comparison covers more than the leaderboard\u8217's own set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Where each number is measured.} The paper reports two environments. The in-house panel (\u167 ?3.1\u8211-\u167 ?3.6) runs on the Apple Silicon laptop described in \u167 ?2.5. The Open Problems results (\u167 ?3.3) run on AWS {\f1 r7i.8xlarge} instances (32 vCPU, 247 GB) through OP\u8217's own Nextflow pipeline, two tasks at a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Accuracy} in Table 6 comes from a single AWS run covering all eleven methods, so every score is one harness, one machine, one invocation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Cost} is reported as a ratio to actinn-jax rather than in seconds, because wall-clock on a shared box depends on what else is running: measured {\f1 %cpu} spans 49% to 2338% across these methods, and actinn-jax itself averaged 165 s/dataset when scheduled alongside OP\u8217's seven and 87 s/dataset alongside our four. Ratios taken within one run are stable where absolute times are not, and actinn-jax was included in both runs to join them. Peak memory does not move with contention and is reported as measured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ablations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three cheap interventions characterize {\i when} a gene-space MLP gains or loses against heavier methods, all selectable without test labels:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Input standardization} \u8212- z-score each selected gene by the reference\u8217's frozen mean/std and apply to the query (a scArches-style domain alignment). Opt-in, because it shifts the probability calibration the abstain thresholds are tuned against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Gene budget} \u8212- Open Problems feeds every method 1,000 HVGs; we sweep wider panels and select per dataset by {\b held-out reference cross-validation}, which is label-free with respect to the test set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Protein-embedding featurization} (negative control). A CPU-only featurization in the style of {\b Universal Cell Embeddings (UCE)} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10689-z"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Rosen 2026]
}}}
: an expression-weighted mean of ESM2 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ade2574"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Lin 2023]
}}}
 protein-language-model gene embeddings. It tests whether a foundation model\u8217's value survives a cheap pooling shortcut.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Results\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Sections 3.1\u8211-3.3 measure methods: eleven trained annotators over eight splits, two pretrained annotators on the splits where that comparison is possible, and an independent run of actinn-jax through the Open Problems label-projection benchmark. \u167 ?3.4 measures how those costs move with reference size, query size and label cardinality. \u167 ?3.5 builds references from public corpora and hands a query from a broad pass to a tissue-specific one. \u167 ?3.6 covers what a confidence threshold does when a query contains types the reference does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Accuracy and cost across the panel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Accuracy is the mean over the {\b five shared-vocabulary datasets} (lung_cross excluded \u8212- its exact-match accuracy is a vocabulary artifact, see the \u8224 ? note below; means over all six are ~0.08 lower for every method, and reorder only scArches and actinn-jax, by 0.0004); macro-F1 is the mean over all six non-brain datasets, ontology concordance the mean over the four of those that carry Cell Ontology ids; cost is the mean per query (Table 3). The two brain splits are excluded from these aggregates and reported per dataset in Table 4, because the columns below come from one measurement pass on an idle machine and brain was run afterwards. Adding brain at subclass level raises every method by 0.024\u8211-0.043 and reorders nothing. Adding both brain splits gives accuracies over eight of scANVI 0.857, linear-anova-pca 0.857, scArches 0.853, CellTypist 0.848, {\b actinn-jax 0.840}, SVM 0.835, ProtoCloud 0.817, SingleR 0.811, kNN 0.792, scTOP 0.775, scmap-cluster 0.706 \u8212- which moves actinn-jax from fourth to fifth, behind CellTypist:\par}
