{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 4.} Per-dataset accuracy: actinn-jax against whichever method leads that dataset. \u8224 ? on lung_cross the exact-match score is a vocabulary artifact, so ontology concordance is the meaningful column. \u8225 ? the two brain rows are the same nuclei scored at two resolutions of the same taxonomy; see below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No single method leads everywhere: ProtoCloud takes three splits, the linear pipeline two, and actinn-jax, scArches and SingleR one apiece. actinn-jax leads the cross-study liver split \u8212- the hardest generalization regime here, and the one closest to real reference mapping \u8212- and is second on within-dataset liver, while trailing on lung, on the 86-type blood+gut set (\u8722 ?4.2 pt to the linear pipeline), and on small-n pbmc. Outside brain, the spread across the leading methods on any one dataset is 1\u8211-4 points, and scmap-cluster is the weakest on accuracy and macro-F1 throughout (0.23 macro-F1 on liver_cross), though not on ontology concordance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The two brain splits are the same nuclei at two resolutions.} At Allen\u8217's 24 subclasses ten of the eleven methods fall inside 0.017 of each other (0.974\u8211-0.991, with scmap-cluster alone below at 0.901) and no method moves more than 0.003 between repeats, so that split does not separate them. At the 151 clusters the ranking {\b inverts}. SingleR leads at 0.845, having placed eighth of eleven overall; scmap-cluster, last everywhere else at 0.646, reaches 0.810; and actinn-jax falls to 0.741, tenth. The two correlation methods gain where the trained classifiers lose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We checked three explanations for that. Every method ran at the same defaults as on the other six splits. Raising actinn-jax\u8217's 50 training epochs to 150 and then 400 moves it 0.741 \u8594 ? 0.746 \u8594 ? 0.747, so eight times the training buys 0.006. And the reference is not unusually thin: this split gives 72 reference nuclei per class against 89 on blood+gut, where actinn-jax scores 0.860. What remains is cardinality, and the provenance of the labels: {\b cluster-level labels are the output of clustering this same expression space}, which favors methods that classify by distance to a class centroid. Methods also disagree about {\i which} clusters are hard (Supplementary Figure S5): median best-minus-worst recall 0.32 per class, mean pairwise Spearman 0.61, concentrated in the graded {\f1 L2/3 IT}, {\f1 Sst} and {\f1 Vip} families rather than in the rare classes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b \u8224 ? lung_cross exact-match accuracy (~0.35 for every method) is a label-vocabulary artifact, not a transfer failure.} HLCA (reference) and Krasnow (query) were annotated independently and share only {\b 20 of 46 cell-type names}; 26 of Krasnow\u8217's types are absent from HLCA\u8217's vocabulary, so no classifier can emit the exact string. The mismatch is granularity: HLCA\u8217's finer taxonomy has {\f1 alveolar / elicited / lung macrophage} but {\b no generic {\f1 macrophage}} (CL:0000235), while Krasnow labels many cells generic {\f1 macrophage} \u8212- so an HLCA-trained model predicts {\f1 lung macrophage} (CL:1001603) for them, exact-wrong but ontology-correct ({\i lung macrophage is-a macrophage}); likewise Krasnow\u8217's generic {\f1 endothelial cell} (CL:0000115) has no HLCA counterpart. {\b Ontology-aware concordance \u8212- which credits a same-lineage call \u8212- is ~0.75 for the trained methods (0.67\u8211-0.79 across the full panel, highest for ProtoCloud)}, so cross-dataset transfer actually works; exact-match just conflates classifier error with vocabulary/granularity mismatch. This is precisely why we report ontology concordance, and it is a general benchmarking pitfall: {\b cross-dataset exact-match accuracy between independently-annotated atlases is not a meaningful accuracy signal.} The other five datasets use a single shared label vocabulary for reference and query (a split of one atlas, or HLiCA\u8217's harmonized annotations), so their exact scores are unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1525\pich763\picwgoal15685\pichgoal7848 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 1.} Eleven methods over seven splits (lung_cross excluded, see the \u8224 ? note above). {\i A:} accuracy as the {\b gap to whichever method leads that split}. Raw accuracy is dominated by how hard a split is \u8212- 0.94 on pbmc against 0.36 on cross-dataset lung \u8212- so a range drawn over raw accuracy would measure the datasets rather than the methods; zero is that split\u8217's leader, and {\f1 leads N} counts the splits a method wins. {\i B:} fit + predict on the same splits, log scale. Diamonds are means over splits, small dots the individual splits, and the line runs from the method\u8217's worst split to its best. Ranges over the three repeats {\i within} a split are too small to draw here: only scArches (0.024), SVM (0.023) and scANVI (0.015) move more than 0.012, and six of the eleven are exactly deterministic. Per-split detail, unnormalized and with those repeat ranges, is Supplementary Figure S11.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Six methods sit within {\b 0.035} of the per-split leader on average while spanning {\b 2.5 s to 51 s}, and the full panel spans {\b 0.4 s to 111 s}. scANVI and the {\b tuned linear pipeline} are the closest to the leader \u8212- mean gap 0.012 and 0.013, with the linear pipeline leading two splits \u8212- and one of them costs 47 s against the other\u8217's 2.5 s. kNN and scTOP are cheaper still, at 0.4 s and 1.8 s, and give up 0.078 and 0.094, so they hold a different corner of the frontier rather than sitting inside it. {\b actinn-jax} sits mid-panel: mean gap 0.030 at 10.0 s, leading the cross-study liver split. What separates it is not on this plot but on the scaling axes of \u167 ?3.4 \u8212- predict time flat in reference size, and a bounded memory margin over the linear pipeline that holds to atlas scale.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every method\u8217's spread across splits is larger than the distance between the leading methods: scmap-cluster spans 0.035 to 0.451, SingleR 0.000 to 0.191, actinn-jax 0.000 to 0.104. A mean over a panel of datasets, including the one in Table 3, summarizes that spread with a single number.\par}
