{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 6.} All eleven methods on the six Open Problems datasets, ordered by accuracy. Bold marks the five components we contributed. {\i cost} is per-dataset wall-clock relative to actinn-jax (\u167 ?2.10), for which 1.00\u215 ? is roughly two minutes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax {\b matches its {\f1 mlp} sibling on accuracy at half the cost}, and {\b beats xgboost on accuracy at 5.5\u215 ? less runtime and 4\u215 ? less memory} (21 vs 81 GB). Everything cheaper \u8212- knn, logistic_regression, naive_bayes, scTOP \u8212- is less accurate, and the one heavyweight is slower and heavier. Nothing here beats it on accuracy and cost together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The tuned linear pipeline loses its \u167 ?3.1 advantage on both axes.} It places third on accuracy (0.828, behind {\f1 mlp} and actinn-jax), and where it fits 7\u215 ? {\i faster} than actinn-jax on the in-house panel it costs {\b 2.7\u215 ? more} here. The input budget explains the reversal: OP hands every method 1,000 HVGs, and an ANOVA\u8594 ?PCA\u8594 ?logistic pipeline pays for the decomposition on every query while a gene-space MLP amortizes it into one fit. CellTypist (7.6\u215 ?) and the SGD-SVM (6.1\u215 ?) shift the same way. Neither a cost ranking nor an accuracy ranking survives a change of feature budget \u8212- which is why we report both panels rather than picking one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 logistic_regression} places fifth on accuracy but first on macro-F1 (0.689), at the second-lowest cost. Across datasets spanning 13 to 160 cell types, accuracy and macro-F1 do not order the panel the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b scTOP collapses on two of the six and is weak on a third.} Its mean (0.581) is not a uniformly weak result but 0.042 on tabula_sapiens, 0.124 on gtex_v9 and 0.495 on immune_cell_atlas against 0.90\u8211-0.99 on the other three. Open Problems hands every method 1,000 HVGs, and scTOP\u8217's rank projection needs genes expressed in an appreciable fraction of cells; after its expression filter only {\b 106 of 1,000} genes survive on gtex_v9 and 210 on tabula_sapiens, against 306\u8211-405 where it works. At that point some test cells retain no counts at all \u8212- 185 of 11,508 on gtex_v9 \u8212- and cannot be placed; they are scored as wrong rather than dropped, so 0.124 is a floor. This is the same limitation \u167 ?3.1 reports from the other direction: scTOP is built for small, low-cardinality problems, and a fixed 1,000-HVG budget over 53 and 160 types is neither.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Three cheap ablations, and their limits.} (i) {\i Input standardization} \u8212- z-scoring genes to the reference\u8217's frozen mean/std, a CPU-only domain-alignment inspired by scArches reference surgery \u8212- lifts mean accuracy +0.2 and macro-F1 +1.2 pt (largest on batch-shifted datasets: gtex macro-F1 +7.9). It is {\b off by default} because it shifts the softmax calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against. (ii) {\i Gene budget} \u8212- OP feeds every method 1000 HVGs, which starves a gene-space MLP more than it starves the others. Widening to ~5000 HVGs lifts accuracy on 4 of 6 datasets (immune/gtex +3 pt) and there {\b matches scANVI+scArches\u8217's full-config leaderboard accuracy} (immune 0.891 \u8776 ? 0.892) on CPU in minutes \u8212- the gap to the top method was largely a gene-budget artifact, not the VAE. But more genes is {\b not} a universal win: it {\i regresses} tabula_sapiens by ~10 pt (its 284-cell test batch across 160 fine types overfits reference-specific genes) and saturates hypomap. The budget is {\b selectable without test labels}: held-out {\i reference} cross-validation rises for the datasets that benefit and is the one signal that {\i drops} for tabula_sapiens, and a trivial query-cells-per-class check independently flags it, so the budget can be set deterministically per dataset (Supplementary Figures S12 and S13). (iii) {\i Negative control} \u8212- a CPU-only, UCE-style protein-embedding featurization (expression-weighted mean of ESM2 gene embeddings) does {\b not} help and hurts the hardest case: the pooling discards the per-gene detail that separates fine types, so the value of a foundation model like UCE stays locked in its GPU transformer, not a portable averaging trick.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling: reference size, query size, memory\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1665\pich1105\picwgoal17125\pichgoal11365 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 2.} Fit and predict time against reference size and label cardinality, for the six CPU methods. Fit grows with both axes for every trained method, steeply for CellTypist and the SVM. Predict is flat in reference size for all six, so that flatness is a property of cached inference generally rather than of any one method; the axis that separates them is cardinality, where actinn-jax rises 3.1\u215 ? from 5 to 86 types against the tuned linear pipeline\u8217's 11.0\u215 ?, the two meeting at ~0.23 s. SVM and kNN predict faster than either throughout. scTOP\u8217's smallest-reference point carries one-time import cost and is not a scaling effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fit time grows with reference size and with #cell types for all trained methods \u8212- actinn-jax\u8217's fit goes 2 s \u8594 ? 16 s \u8594 ? 22 s as the reference grows 965 \u8594 ? 14.8k \u8594 ? 24k cells, below CellTypist (7 s \u8594 ? 71 s \u8594 ? 109 s) and SVM (4 s \u8594 ? 46 s \u8594 ? 75 s) at every size in the sweep, and above the tuned linear pipeline (1 s \u8594 ? 7 s \u8594 ? 12 s); kNN does no work at fit time beyond storing the reference, so all of its cost lands on predict, where it is also the least accurate method here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On the other axis, {\b predict stays sub-second for every method across the whole sweep}, and the first thing to say is that flatness in reference size does not distinguish anything: all six move by 1.1\u8211-2.5\u215 ? while the reference grows twenty-five-fold, because a cached model\u8217's inference does not depend on how many cells trained it. Cardinality is where they part. actinn-jax rises 3.1\u215 ? from 5 to 86 types (0.074 \u8594 ? 0.228 s); the tuned linear pipeline starts three times cheaper and rises 11.0\u215 ? (0.022 \u8594 ? 0.244 s), so the two arrive at the same place; scTOP rises 10.5\u215 ?. Neither of the two cheapest is ours \u8212- SVM (0.016\u8211-0.055 s) and kNN (0.018\u8211-0.100 s) predict faster than actinn-jax everywhere, and pay for it in accuracy (Table 3).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What the cached reference buys is therefore not a uniquely flat predict but the right {\i ratio}: a sub-second call recurring against a fit of 12\u8211-109 s that is paid once. That is the regime that matters when one reference serves many queries, and it is what makes chaining several annotation stages practical; it would be as true of the linear pipeline if it cached its scaler, PCA and classifier rather than refitting them per query. (This sweep\u8217's own memory column is process-cumulative and not a clean per-size measurement; the atlas sweep below and the matrix of \u167 ?3.1 both run each method in its own process, and are what the memory numbers come from.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b At atlas scale the ordering changes.} The sweep above stops at 24k cells. Carrying four methods to full atlas size \u8212- 49k reference cells on lung, 47k on the HLiCA liver atlas \u8212- reverses the accuracy ranking (Figure 3A, B). ProtoCloud goes from the weakest method at 3k cells (0.722 lung, 0.581 liver) to the strongest at full scale ({\b 0.976} and {\b 0.905}), clear of the linear pipeline (0.939, 0.863) and actinn-jax (0.936, 0.824), at 19\u215 ? actinn-jax\u8217's CPU fit cost. scTOP gains little from scale on lung (0.819 \u8594 ? 0.846, barely outside its interval) and loses ground on liver (0.657 \u8594 ? 0.583). Peak memory grows for every method, but the spread stays {\b bounded at ~2\u215 ?} rather than widening (Figure 3C): the linear pipeline is heaviest throughout \u8212- 13.2 GB against actinn-jax\u8217's 6.1 GB at 49k lung cells, a 2.15\u215 ? ratio \u8212- and scTOP crosses over from lightest to heaviest as its rank processing densifies (9.3 vs 6.5 GB at 47k liver cells). Conclusions drawn from subsampled references do not transfer to atlas scale, in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw3463\pich850\picwgoal25058\pichgoal6150 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 3.} Accuracy and peak memory against reference size, four methods carried to full atlas scale on two datasets. {\i A:} lung, 3k \u8594 ? 49k reference cells. {\i B:} the HLiCA liver atlas, 2.7k \u8594 ? 47k, an independent replication of the same reversal. {\i C:} peak memory over both sweeps. Error bars on {\i A} and {\i B} are 95% binomial intervals on the query, which grows with the reference (1,035 to 16,398 cells on lung), so they narrow from left to right; each point is a single run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Annotating an atlas costs less than building one.} The sweeps above grow the reference and hold the query fixed. The reverse case is the one a user meets most often \u8211- a reference built once, then pointed at everything \u8211- and it is where a flat inference profile should pay. Holding the reference at 17,753 cells drawn from seven HLiCA studies and growing the query from 50k cells to the entire 524,699-cell atlas, actinn-jax annotates the whole atlas in {\b 31 s} (Figure 4A). Accuracy on the withheld eighth study is 0.720 and does not move across the range, as it should not: the subsets are nested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tuned linear pipeline stays about twice as slow throughout, annotating the same atlas in 67 s, and the gap narrows as the query grows: actinn-jax gives up 21% of its rate across the range, 21,400 \u8594 ? 16,800 cells/s, against the linear pipeline\u8217's 10%, 8,700 \u8594 ? 7,900 (Figure 4B). The advantage is 2.4\u215 ? at 50k cells and 2.1\u215 ? at the full atlas. It is the cheaper method whose per-cell cost drifts upward, which is the opposite of what a flat-inference argument predicts, so the claim this axis supports is a roughly twofold constant factor rather than flatness. The flat-inference result in \u167 ?3.1 holds along the reference axis, which is a different axis from this one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Which of the two is cheaper depends on how many cells are annotated at once.} The two measurements do not overlap \u8212- every query in this sweep holds at least 50,000 cells, every query in Table 3 at most 13,550 \u8212- but across both the ordering turns over once, with query size, at around 2,500 cells:\par}
