{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Below the crossover the linear pipeline is 1.5\u8211-1.7\u215 ? faster; above it actinn-jax is, by 1.2\u215 ? at 2,694 cells, 1.9\u215 ? at 13,550 and 2.1\u8211-2.4\u215 ? across the atlas sweep. Part of the small-query end is one-time cost: calling {\f1 predict} repeatedly on one liver_intra model and query settles from 0.24\u8211-0.30 s on the first call to 0.12\u8211-0.13 s by the third ({\f1 predict_overhead_probe.py}), so roughly 0.12\u8211-0.17 s is compilation and warm-up \u8212- about half the first call at 1,332 cells and a tenth of it at 13,550. The harness times the first call, which is what a one-shot annotation costs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The large-query end has a different mechanism. actinn-jax\u8217's throughput rises with the query, from 7,900 cells/s at 657 cells to 24,000 at 13,550, settling at 16,800\u8211-21,500 across the atlas sweep; the linear pipeline holds 11,500\u8211-14,000 on the small queries and falls to 7,900\u8211-8,700 at atlas scale, because its dense feature block leaves cache: 20,000 genes over 1,332 cells is 107 MB, and the same 20,000 genes over a 50,000-cell block is 4 GB and bounded by memory bandwidth. A benchmark reporting one query size cannot show any of this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Peak memory does not separate them at all.} Both land at 33.2 GB on the full atlas (Figure 4C), because on this axis peak memory measures holding the query rather than running the method: at the largest size, prediction added nothing measurable on top of the resident query for either. A caller streaming from disk would pay less, and would pay it equally. The ~2\u215 ? memory band reported above is likewise a {\i reference}-axis result and does not carry to this one; only the reference axis separates these methods on memory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two conditions of this measurement. Prediction in our linear adapter blocks the query into 50,000-cell chunks. Unblocked, it densifies 20,000 genes for the whole query at once, which needs roughly 126 GB at the full atlas and does not complete on a 51 GB machine \u8212- a property of the wrapper rather than of the recipe. Blocking leaves every prediction bit-identical, and is verified to. Second, these are laptop numbers, so each point is the fastest of three runs: a competing process can only add time, and stalls on this machine are large and sporadic rather than small and Gaussian \u8211- one repeat turned a 50 s fit into 975 s, and no mean over three runs would survive that. Peak memory takes the largest of three instead, since {\f1 ru_maxrss} counts resident pages and therefore reports {\i less} than a run needed whenever the OS evicts under pressure. Per-run values are released with the harness so the spread is inspectable rather than summarised away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2657\pich916\picwgoal19226\pichgoal6628 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 4.} Cost against query size \u8212- 50,000 to 524,699 cells \u8212- with the reference fixed at 17,753 cells. Table 3\u8217's queries are all smaller than the leftmost point here (657 to 13,550 cells), so the two do not overlap. {\i A:} wall-clock to annotate the query. {\i B:} throughput, which declines 21% for actinn-jax and 10% for the linear pipeline, narrowing the advantage from 2.4\u215 ? to 2.1\u215 ?. {\i C:} peak memory, which does not distinguish them \u8212- on this axis it measures holding the query, not running the method. Three runs per point on a shared laptop: {\i A} and {\i B} report the fastest run, since contention can only add time, and {\i C} the largest peak, since resident-set size understates a run that the OS has partly evicted. Per-run values are in {\f1 results_query_scaling.csv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Building references, and the broad\u8594 ?focused workflow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What actinn-jax is built around is not a single classifier but a {\b two-pass workflow} that matches how annotation is actually done: get a broad call fast, then sharpen it where it matters. Because every stage is a cached {\f1 ReferenceModel} with sub-second, memory-bounded inference (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?3.4), the whole workflow runs on a laptop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2681\pich988\picwgoal19400\pichgoal7149 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 5.} The workflow on a withheld HLiCA liver study (3,396 cells), same embedding throughout. {\i Left:} the census reference spreads 144 of its 798 labels over the query (concordance {\b 0.34}) \u8212- it is not trying to name subtypes. {\i Center:} those calls resolved to tissue through the reference\u8217's per-class tissue map; {\b 76%} of tissue-specific calls say liver against 4% for the next candidate, which is the decision that selects the next reference. {\i Right of center:} the 36-type liver reference re-annotates the same cells at {\b 0.73}, now tracking the clusters. {\i Right:} the study\u8217's own labels on the shared palette. Both models are leakage-free for this query: the focused reference is trained only on the six non-withheld studies. Abstention is off in all panels, so the numbers measure labeling rather than coverage; \u167 ?3.6 covers abstain separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The broad pass \u8212- census-scale.} A ~800-type human reference built from the CELLxGENE census gives any query a first-pass annotation across the whole body, with a {\b calibrated abstain threshold} so out-of-reference cells are flagged rather than force-labeled (\u167 ?3.6). This is the orientation pass \u8212- broad coverage, deliberately not fine-grained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The broad pass can be built from a model instead of from labels.} The census route above needs a foundation model on a GPU to discover its hierarchy. A pretrained pan-human annotator already has one \u8212- Pan-human Azimuth publishes an 8-level typology with every node mapped to a Cell Ontology term \u8212- so labeling a corpus with it and training actinn-jax on those labels transfers both the vocabulary and the structure. That build needs {\b no GPU and no labeled input}, only raw human counts: under ten minutes of CPU on 85k cells drawn from three atlases plus a census-wide sample. On 3,396 withheld cross-study liver cells:\par}
{
