{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 9.} Ontology concordance within each agreement tier, three broad references on identical cells, in three tissues: withheld cross-study liver (3,396 cells, 34 truth types), the Krasnow lung atlas (65,662, 46) and the Allen human middle temporal gyrus (156,285, 18).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One thing replicates in all three tissues: cells the references disagree about are annotated far less reliably than cells they concur on, for every reference. The census model spans 0.690 to 0.212 across the liver partition, 0.934 to 0.059 across lung and 0.839 to 0.117 across brain, with the same direction for the other two. That all three improve {\i together} is what makes the partition useful \u8212- agreement selects cells that are unambiguous rather than cells one model happens to get right \u8212- and unlike accuracy it can be computed on a query whose answers are unknown, for the price of three sub-second calls.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What does not replicate is how much of a query the agreeing set covers: 23% on liver, 48% on lung, 94% on brain. Brain explains the spread rather than extending it. That query\u8217's Cell Ontology annotation uses {\b 18 terms for a region whose own taxonomy, {\f1 CCN201908210}, defines 154 cell sets} \u8212- 151 of them present in this data, and the label set of the fine brain split in \u167 ?3.1 \u8212- while 55% of its cells fall in one class, {\f1 L2/3-6 intratelencephalic projecting glutamatergic neuron}. Concordances near 0.98 and agreement at 94% are what a coarse truth vocabulary produces, not what an easy tissue produces. The partition reports the resolution of the annotation it is scored against, which is a property of the query rather than of the method: it reports how much of a query is unambiguous at the resolution that query is annotated to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The consensus {\i label} is not the prize in any of the three, and brain shows why the rule itself is weak. Taking the most specific call the agreeing references support scores 0.365 on liver against the best single reference\u8217's 0.408, 0.828 on lung against 0.831, and 0.837 on brain against 0.987. That last gap is large because \u8220"most specific agreeing call\u8221" lets one reference\u8217's confident, lineage-compatible but wrong specificity override two correct coarser calls: on the 94% of brain cells where all three agree, the two strong references score 0.997 and the consensus built from them scores 0.837. The partition separates reliable calls from unreliable ones on all three queries; the consensus label does not beat a single reference on any of them, under this rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Agreement is defined by the Cell Ontology.} Two calls agree when CL says one subsumes the other, a relation this analysis inherits rather than sets. Where CL is coarse or incomplete, two references naming the same population can be scored as disagreeing; where it is deep, a pair of calls can agree at a resolution neither reference meant to assert. The comparison was possible at all only because Pan-human Azimuth publishes a CL term for every node of its typology; an annotator with no crosswalk cannot enter this analysis regardless of how good its labels are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The alternative design targets exactly this problem, and looking at it closely shows why the substitution is not free. The Common Cell type Nomenclature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.59928"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Miller 2020]
}}}
 gives each taxonomy\u8217's cell sets stable accessions ({\f1 CS[taxonomy id]_[n]}) plus a curated alias layer, treating CL as one alignment target rather than as the coordinate system. In the published human middle temporal gyrus taxonomy {\f1 CCN201908210}, 154 cell sets carry an accession and a structure tag \u8212- {\f1 UBERON:0002771}, the gyrus itself \u8212- but {\b no cell-type ontology id at all}; the field that matches cell sets across taxonomies, the aligned alias, is populated for {\b 23 of the 154}. Of those 23, eight match a CL term name or synonym by exact string ({\f1 L2/3 IT}, {\f1 L5 IT}, {\f1 L5/6 NP}, {\f1 L6 CT}, {\f1 L6 IT}, {\f1 Lamp5}, {\f1 OPC}, {\f1 Sst Chodl}); most of the rest are abbreviations such as {\f1 Astro}, {\f1 Oligo} and {\f1 Endo} whose expansions do exist in CL, so the true overlap is larger than a string test finds and is exactly as large as someone is willing to curate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The 151-type brain split of \u167 ?3.1 uses those cell sets as its label set. Because they carry no ontology id, calls at that resolution can only be checked against the taxonomy\u8217's own strings: that split is scored by exact match alone, and the ranking it produces \u8212- correlation methods first, trained classifiers last \u8212- cannot be cross-checked against the ontology-aware column used elsewhere in this paper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That is the substantive difference. CL supplies a {\i total} subsumption relation \u8212- every pair of terms is comparable, at whatever granularity CL happens to encode \u8212- which is what makes an agreement partition computable at all. CCN supplies exact provenance, which CL cannot: it records which taxonomy, which algorithm and which publication a label came from. An agreement relation built on CCN accessions alone would be undefined for most pairs of cell sets, because accessions are deliberately taxonomy-scoped rather than shared. The two systems answer different questions, and a workflow that compares annotations across references needs both: CL to decide whether two calls are compatible, CCN to say what each call actually was.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Abstention and novel cell types\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Holding out 9 of 36 cell types entirely from the HLiCA liver reference (so 1,350 query cells are genuinely out-of-distribution), we sweep a confidence threshold over every method that returns a per-cell confidence. Eight do; for scANVI and scArches the confidence is the maximum class posterior from the model\u8217's soft prediction. SVM, SingleR and scPRINT return no per-cell confidence and cannot be swept, and scmap-cluster has a single native \u8220"unassigned\u8221" decision rather than a threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2696\pich1357\picwgoal19508\pichgoal9819 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 7.} What a confidence threshold does to each of the eight methods that return a per-cell confidence, with 9 of 36 cell types held out of the liver reference so 1,350 query cells are out-of-distribution. Every quantity is a fraction, so all three share one axis: accuracy on kept cells, coverage, and the share of held-out-type cells flagged. The five that work share a shape \u8212- accuracy and novelty rising as coverage falls \u8212- and the three that do not each fail visibly and differently. Per-threshold values are in {\f1 results_rejection.csv}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Five of the eight give a threshold that does something; three do not.} actinn-jax, scArches, scANVI, the linear pipeline and kNN all trade coverage for accuracy across the range. The other three fail in different ways: CellTypist\u8217's probabilities are saturated near 0 or 1, so every threshold from 0.3 to 0.9 lands on one operating point; scTOP\u8217's projection score is not a calibrated probability and discards all but 6% of the query by p\u8805 ?0.5; ProtoCloud\u8217's ambiguity flag barely moves until 0.9.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Among the five that work, abstain quality does not separate them the way cost does (Figure 8). actinn-jax and scArches are effectively tied \u8212- at p\u8805 ?0.9, 0.969 accuracy on 66% of cells with 73% of novel cells flagged against 0.983 on 61% with 71% flagged \u8212- and scANVI is close behind. kNN reaches the highest novelty detection (83%) by keeping only 36% of the query, which is a different operating regime rather than a better one, and the linear pipeline is the weakest here, flagging 46% of novel cells at the threshold where actinn-jax flags 73%. The distinction worth drawing is therefore not that actinn-jax abstains better than the deep methods but that it abstains as well for 0.24 s of predict time against scArches\u8217's 11.9 s and scANVI\u8217's 58.7 s (Table 3) \u8212- the same accuracy-at-a-fraction-of-the-cost pattern \u167 ?3.1 reports, applied to the mechanism the workflow of \u167 ?3.5 routes on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2218\pich996\picwgoal16049\pichgoal7207 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 8.} The same sweep read as a trade-off, which is what compares methods to each other rather than to themselves. {\i Left:} accuracy on kept cells against the fraction kept \u8212- the five usable methods lie along one band, which is the basis for calling their abstain quality tied. {\i Right:} the share of held-out-type cells flagged as the threshold rises. The three methods Figure 7 shows failing appear here as curves that go nowhere along one axis or the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Method choice is now dominated by cost, not accuracy.} The four leading methods are separated by 0.008 in mean accuracy and by ~250\u215 ? in predict time (\u167 ?3.1), so for most annotation jobs the decision is made on the cost axes. On laptop-sized references the tuned linear pipeline is the best of them on accuracy-per-second (0.839, fitting in 2.5 s); at atlas scale ProtoCloud is the most accurate by a clear margin (0.976 vs 0.936 at 49k lung cells; 0.905 vs 0.824 at 47k liver cells; 0.998 vs 0.992 at 46k brain cells), having been below the cluster on the subsampled matrix. actinn-jax\u8217's place in that picture rests on a cost profile with two properties that matter for repeated use rather than for a single labeling run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Footprint.} Inference is {\b sub-second} \u8212- flat in reference size, and rising 3.1\u215 ? with cardinality while staying under a quarter of a second (\u167 ?3.4) \u8212- so a query costs the same against a 1k-cell reference and a 49k-cell one. Peak memory is unremarkable: 2.4 GB mean on small references, where seven of the ten other methods are lighter, and at {\b atlas scale} it is the third of four we carried there \u8212- ProtoCloud is the lightest on all three tissues (7.3 GB at 49k lung, 8.6 at 47k liver, 9.1 at 46k brain), then scTOP (7.5 / 9.3 / 10.6), then actinn-jax (8.8 / 9.6 / 14.5), with the linear pipeline last (16.6 / 17.0 / 20.9). What actinn-jax has on that axis is a {\b 1.4\u8211-1.9\u215 ? margin over the linear pipeline}, bounded rather than widening, and a profile that stays flat where the linear pipeline\u8217's densified feature block grows. Combined with the train-once/map-many cache, a reused reference pays fit once and then only the flat predict cost, where the linear pipeline refits scaler/PCA/classifier for every query.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Those two properties are what the workflows are built on.} The practical gains come not from the flat classifier but from the {\b broad\u8594 ?focused} pipeline (\u167 ?3.5): a census-scale broad model with a calibrated abstain routes a query to a small focused reference that re-annotates at full resolution (cross-study liver 0.23/0.58 \u8594 ? 0.72/0.86), with zonation as a further stage. The pipeline is only usable because each hop is cheap \u8212- a flat sub-second call against a cached model, at bounded memory \u8212- so several models can be chained, and a large reference kept resident, on a laptop without a GPU. A method that is a few points more accurate but pays a full fit per query, or a multi-gigabyte dense expansion per stage, does not compose the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Where it loses, and why.} Much of what looked like a deficit of the {\i model} on Open Problems was a deficit of its {\b input}. The benchmark hands every method 1,000 highly variable genes, a far harsher constraint on a model reading gene space directly than on one that learns a compressed representation first, and at a fair gene budget the gene-space MLP {\i matches the leaderboard\u8217's top method} on the datasets both complete. Widen the gene panel and most of the gap closes, so the quantity being measured was partly \u8220"how well does each method tolerate a narrow input\u8221", not \u8220"which model class is stronger\u8221".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The genuine weaknesses are narrower. actinn-jax trails on small references and very-few-cells-per-type sets (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?5), and it is sensitive to input budget under domain shift: more genes help most datasets but overfit a tiny, fine-grained, shifted query (tabula_sapiens). The two cheapest levers are principled and label-free \u8212- {\b standardization} (a scArches-style domain alignment) and a {\b gene budget chosen by cross-validation on the reference alone} \u8212- and neither peeks at test labels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Foundation models.} Their {\b zero-shot labels} are the weakest option in both benchmarks (scPRINT, scGPT, UCE \u8776 ? random); their {\b embeddings/structure} are where the value lies, and our two-stage hierarchy uses exactly that. A cheap CPU shortcut to a foundation-model representation (protein-embedding pooling) did {\i not} transfer (\u167 ?3.3), underscoring that the value is in the trained transformer, not a portable trick. The concurrent {\b Pan-human Azimuth} effort reaches the same conclusion from a far larger curated corpus. Their 7M-parameter supervised hierarchical MLP over a 5,055-gene panel yields cleaner annotations than scGPT and SCimilarity; they report those models\u8217' labels as fragmented and mis-transferred. Their stated theme \u8212- that training-data quality and organization matter as much as architecture or scale \u8212- is one of the two claims we draw here, and their finding that accuracy {\b saturates past ~5M training cells} supports it further. The other claim, that zero-shot foundation-model labels are the weaker option, they reach independently. Both come from a group with no stake in our conclusion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i When extra expressivity pays.} Two lines of evidence bear on this. First, {\b ProtoCloud} \u8212- a prototype-based, self-explaining VAE with built-in uncertainty and gene-level attribution, a strictly richer model than ours \u8212- does not sit a fixed distance above or below us. Where it lands depends on how much reference data it is given: below the top cluster on the subsampled matrix, ahead on lung, and the strongest method of all at atlas scale (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?3.4), at ~8\u215 ? actinn-jax\u8217's CPU fit time (176 vs 22 s mean). The richer model pays off where it has the data to support it, and not before \u8212- which is an argument for matching model capacity to reference size, not for preferring small models everywhere. Second, and more broadly, Souza & Mehta {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.02.11.705358"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Souza & Mehta 2026]
}}}
 report that {\b parameter-free linear representations} match or exceed single-cell foundation models across cross-species transfer, human cell-type classification and disease-state prediction. On Tabula Sapiens 2.0 their normalize\u8594 ?ANOVA\u8594 ?PCA\u8594 ?logistic-regression pipeline reaches mean macro-F1 0.899, against 0.907\u8211-0.910 for TranscriptFormer variants. Their explanation is geometric: the biologically realized cell manifold is well approximated by a {\b linear subspace}, so once noise is suppressed performance {\i saturates} and extra expressivity buys little. That is a principled account of why a four-layer MLP over normalized gene space stays competitive, and it matches our own finding that the residual gap to heavier methods was mostly {\b the size of the input each method was given rather than the class of model} (\u167 ?3.3). They further find that simple methods hold up {\i best} out-of-distribution \u8212- on novel cell types and unseen organisms. That matters for the abstain mechanism of \u167 ?3.6, which is only useful if low confidence tracks genuine novelty rather than model noise. A method that degrades gradually on unfamiliar data still assigns lower confidence to the cells it is getting wrong, so a threshold separates them; a method that degrades abruptly can be confidently wrong, and no threshold helps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Practical guidance.} For a {\b one-off annotation} on laptop-sized data, use the {\b tuned linear pipeline} (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? PCA \u8594 ? logistic regression): it is the most accurate-per-second method here. With a {\b real atlas and time to train}, {\b ProtoCloud} is the most accurate method we benchmarked (0.976 at 49k cells), and the GPU it targets makes its 19\u215 ? CPU fit cost much less punishing in practice. {\b scTOP} suits small, low-cardinality problems, where it is by far the cheapest (~1 s) and competitive. {\b actinn-jax} fits the cases its cost profile is shaped for: when {\b memory is the binding constraint} (~2\u215 ? lighter than the linear pipeline at scale, with memory-bounded chunked inference), when a reference is {\b reused} across many queries (the cached model amortizes the fit), and when the job is the {\b multi-stage workflow} rather than a single label \u8212- broad\u8594 ?focused hand-off, tissue-aware refinement, calibrated abstain, novel-cell-type screening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\b broad pass specifically, prefer a purpose-built pan-human model}: {\b Pan-human Azimuth} is a pretrained hierarchical classifier over a harmonized organism-wide typology (8 levels, 382 leaf types, trained on 9.7M curated cells), with abstention learned rather than thresholded and an {\b expected calibration error (ECE)} of 0.0044 \u8212- meaning its stated confidence sits within half a percentage point of its observed accuracy \u8212- running at ~1,000 cells/s on a laptop. It is better resourced than our census-built reference and we make no claim to improve on its annotations. What we do with it instead is {\b use it}: distilling its labels and its hierarchy into actinn-jax produces a broad-pass model of comparable accuracy at six to nine times its throughput, built without a GPU and without labeled data (\u167 ?3.5). A curated pan-human model is the right thing to start from; a small fast model is the right thing to iterate with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What no fixed typology can do is the {\b hand-off}: it cannot re-annotate into a user\u8217's own focused label set (the HLiCA liver reference, cross-study 0.23/0.58 \u8594 ? 0.72/0.86) or resolve states below a leaf (hepatocyte zonation). ProtoCloud likewise provides uncertainty, gene attribution and a refinement path (fine-tuning a pretrained model). What survives as distinct to actinn-jax is therefore not the breadth of the broad reference but {\b the chain it enables} \u8212- start pan-human, re-annotate into label sets no pretrained model carries, screen what none of them claims \u8212- at a cost that keeps every stage on a laptop. The distillation recipe is not specific to this teacher or to humans: it needs a pretrained annotator whose typology can be read off its outputs, so extending the same entry point to other organisms is a matter of finding one (or building the broad-pass reference from a labeled census slice, as \u167 ?3.5\u8217's census route does).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What the speed is for.} Annotation is almost never the result; it is the step before the biology, and it is usually run more than once \u8212- the labels change when a reference is swapped, a threshold moves, a new sample arrives, or a cluster turns out to be two things. The argument of this paper is not that a faster classifier produces better labels; at equal data the leading methods differ by at most 0.008 in accuracy (\u167 ?3.1). It is that when a stage costs a second instead of a minute, and a chain of stages fits in memory on the machine already on the desk, the loop from question to annotated data to the next question closes in an afternoon rather than across a queue. That is the contribution we would defend: not a better number in a table, but a workflow \u8212- start from the best pan-human model available, refine into the labels a given study actually uses, and flag what neither covers \u8212- that a working scientist can run, inspect, and run again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Single hardware family for the in-house panel (Apple Silicon); no discrete-GPU numbers there. The deep and foundation methods are written for CUDA and we would expect them to be faster on it, but we did not measure that \u8212- it is outside the \u8220"runs-on-a-laptop\u8221" question, and every timing here should be read as this hardware\u8217's. The \u167 ?3.3 controlled run covers the {\b CPU tier} on one cloud box; the GPU/R methods there are reported from OP\u8217's own cloud-CI trace (indicative). A same-hardware GPU-tier run to fold those into a single controlled table is the natural extension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Wall-clock comparisons across methods are only meaningful at matched concurrency.} Measured {\f1 %cpu} on the OP harness spans 49% to 2338% across methods \u8212- some are single-threaded, some saturate 23 cores \u8212- so the same method timed at two concurrency settings differs by up to 12\u215 ?, and any wall-clock ranking taken at high concurrency silently ranks threading and scheduler contention alongside algorithm. Table 6 therefore reports cost as a ratio within a run, anchored by a method common to both (\u167 ?2.10) \u8212- the anchor itself moved 165 s \u8594 ? 87 s between them, which is the size of the effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The cross-method comparison is human only}; eight splits per benchmark; GPU foundation models beyond scPRINT/UCE (scGPT, Geneformer, popV) not run locally. The bundled references now cover mouse (\u167 ?3.5), but no {\i method comparison} was run on mouse data \u8212- the pan-mouse result establishes that the reference-building route works on a second organism, not that actinn-jax\u8217's standing against other methods carries over to it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b actinn-jax\u8217's standing is worse at very fine granularity.} On the 151-type brain cluster split it places tenth of eleven (0.741 against SingleR\u8217's 0.845, \u167 ?3.1), and the two correlation methods that trail everywhere else lead there. The panel covers one split at that cardinality, and it runs opposite to the 86-type blood+gut result; Table 3\u8217's aggregate does not cover annotation at cluster resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab actinn-jax needs more cells/type than linear methods to reach parity (\u167 ?3.1); on very small references it trails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The distilled broad-pass reference inherits a vocabulary, not a calibration.} Pan-human Azimuth\u8217's abstention is trained (and its ECE measured at 0.0044); the distilled student learns hard labels only, so it carries none of that \u8212- its confidence barely separates right from wrong (\u167 ?3.5). Recalibrating it, or distilling from soft targets, is the obvious next step and would need a loss change in the package. Its evaluation is also two withheld {\i atlases}, not withheld tissue: the census-wide corpus spans 376 tissues, so nothing here measures behavior on biology the corpus never saw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The gene budget is dataset-dependent, not a free parameter.} More genes help most references but {\i overfit} a tiny, fine-grained, domain-shifted query (tabula_sapiens \u8722 ?10 pt); our reference-cross-validation + cells-per-class selection rule is validated on 6 datasets with a single clean failure case, so it is directional evidence, not a tuned threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Standardization is opt-in}, not default, because it shifts probability calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against; combining the two cleanly would require re-tuning those thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Our classical tier (SVM, kNN, CellTypist) is untuned, and a tuned linear baseline beats actinn-jax.} Souza & Mehta\u8217's pipeline (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? standardize \u8594 ? PCA(220) \u8594 ? logistic regression) leads the matrix on accuracy (0.839 vs 0.831) and macro-F1 (0.699 vs 0.683) while fitting 7\u215 ? faster on this panel\u8217's hardware (\u167 ?2.5), with its largest margin on the 86-type blood+gut set (+4.2 pt). Wall-clock ratios between a JAX model and a scikit-learn pipeline shift with the CPU and the threading available, so read them as this machine\u8217's, not as constants. The \u167 ?3.1 margins over the classical tier should therefore be read as margins over {\i untuned} baselines; a like-for-like tuning effort on SVM or CellTypist would likely narrow them further. scTOP is cheaper still to fit but competitive only at small, low cardinality \u8212- its own best macro-F1 on pbmc (0.837), degrading to 0.644 on liver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b actinn-jax\u8217's memory advantage is bounded, and the linear pipeline scales further than a naive extrapolation implies}. Extrapolating the linear pipeline\u8217's small-reference footprint overstates its atlas-scale peak by ~2.4\u215 ? \u8212- the measured figure is {\b 13.2 GB} at 49k reference cells \u8212- because its feature-selection step caps the dense matrix at 20,000 genes rather than the full gene set. The linear/actinn-jax memory ratio is likewise {\b bounded at ~2\u8211-3\u215 ?} (2.15\u215 ? at full scale) rather than widening with data, so there is no scaling cliff and no regime tested where the linear pipeline stops being usable. Memory in this setting should be measured, not extrapolated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The main matrix uses subsampled references, and its ranking does not survive atlas scale.} Carried to 49k lung and 47k liver reference cells, ProtoCloud moves from the weakest method to the strongest and scTOP crosses from the lightest to heavier than actinn-jax (\u167 ?3.4, Figure 3). The accuracy ordering of Table 3 describes laptop-sized references, which is the regime this paper is about; at atlas scale the ordering is the one in Figure 3.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Annotation only.} Cross-species transfer and disease-state prediction \u8212- the tasks where Souza & Mehta find the largest foundation-model deficits, and where a fast method would be most attractive \u8212- are outside this benchmark\u8217's scope (human, within/cross-dataset annotation).\sa180\par}
